# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Browser (VITE_*) — bundled into the client
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Organizer's PERSONAL UPI details, used to generate the payment QR.
#
# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in real values there. Real values are
# intentionally never committed; .gitignore already excludes .env.local.
#
# Restart `pnpm dev` after changing these - Vite reads them at startup.
#
# Never put an authentication secret in a VITE_* variable: everything prefixed
# with VITE_ is bundled into the browser.

VITE_UPI_ID=
VITE_UPI_PAYEE_NAME=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Browser - offline device authorization PUBLIC key (VITE_ is correct here)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The P-256 PUBLIC key the browser uses to VERIFY a signed offline
# authorization lease, as SPKI DER in base64.
#
# This one is MEANT to be in the bundle. It is the verification half of an
# asymmetric pair: it can check a lease and cannot mint one. The signing half
# lives only on the server as EVENT_DEVICE_OFFLINE_PRIVATE_KEY_PKCS8_B64 and
# must never be VITE_ prefixed.
#
# Asymmetric precisely so this value can be public. An HMAC would require the
# signing secret in browser code, and anyone with the bundle could then forge
# Registration or Prizes access for themselves.
#
# Generate the pair (never against Production, never commit the output):
#
#   openssl ecparam -name prime256v1 -genkey -noout -out device-offline.pem
#   openssl pkcs8 -topk8 -nocrypt -in device-offline.pem -outform DER \
#     | base64 | tr -d '\n'     # -> EVENT_DEVICE_OFFLINE_PRIVATE_KEY_PKCS8_B64
#   openssl ec -in device-offline.pem -pubout -outform DER \
#     | base64 | tr -d '\n'     # -> VITE_EVENT_DEVICE_OFFLINE_PUBLIC_KEY_SPKI_B64
#   rm device-offline.pem
#
# Leaving it unset is a valid state: device sign-in still works online, the
# page reports offline authorization unavailable, and nothing fails open.

VITE_EVENT_DEVICE_OFFLINE_PUBLIC_KEY_SPKI_B64=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server only - Google Sheets sync (NEVER prefix these with VITE_)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These are read only by the Vercel Function at /api/sync-registration. The
# service-account private key must never reach the browser, so it must never be
# given a VITE_ prefix and the service-account JSON file must never be committed.
#
# All four are required. If any is missing the endpoint fails closed with
# `sync-not-configured` and touches nothing.
#
# GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_PRIVATE_KEY may contain escaped \n sequences; the
# server converts them to real newlines.
#
# SYNC_ALLOWED_ORIGIN is the exact application origin allowed to POST, e.g.
# http://localhost:3000 locally. See the README: this is not authentication.

GOOGLE_SHEETS_SPREADSHEET_ID=
GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_EMAIL=
GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_PRIVATE_KEY=
SYNC_ALLOWED_ORIGIN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server only - sync release interlock (NEVER prefix these with VITE_)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Credentials existing on a deployment is NOT enough to write to the event
# ledger. A git push can create a deployment before a human intended one to be
# live, so writes additionally require this switch AND a matching runtime.
#
# SYNC_WRITE_ENABLED must be exactly `true`. Nothing else enables writes -
# unset, blank, `false`, `TRUE`, `True`, `1`, `yes`, and any padded value such
# as ` true` or `true ` all mean disabled. Whitespace is NOT trimmed.
#
# SYNC_ALLOWED_VERCEL_ENV must be exactly one of: development | preview |
# production. No trimming, no case folding: ` development`, `Production` and
# `staging` all fail closed.
#
# It must exactly equal the runtime VERCEL_ENV, and that value is not trimmed
# either. If VERCEL_ENV is absent, sync fails closed - the runtime cannot be
# confirmed. There is deliberately NO fallback that assumes development.
#
# Deployed Production and Preview runtimes get VERCEL_ENV from Vercel itself.
# LOCALLY, this project's `vercel dev` does NOT expose it to the function, so
# you must supply it yourself when testing guarded sync - see the README.
#
# A whitespace or casing typo is a configuration failure to fix, never
# something the code repairs on your behalf.
#
# When either check fails the endpoint returns the existing safe
# `sync-not-configured` response and touches no Google API. Registration,
# Hold and Issue Badge are unaffected; rows simply stay in the local outbox.
#
# Development (local, disposable test spreadsheet only):
#   SYNC_WRITE_ENABLED=true
#   SYNC_ALLOWED_VERCEL_ENV=development
#
# and start the local server with VERCEL_ENV supplied explicitly:
#   env VERCEL_ENV=development pnpm dlx vercel@latest dev --listen 3001
#
# Preview: prefer NO Google sync credentials at all, and leave writes disabled.
# A preview deployment must never point at the final event spreadsheet.
#
# Production: keep SYNC_WRITE_ENABLED unset until every release gate in
# docs/PRODUCTION_RELEASE_CHECKLIST.md has passed - deployment protection
# included. Only then set it to true.

SYNC_WRITE_ENABLED=
SYNC_ALLOWED_VERCEL_ENV=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server only - operator access (NEVER prefix these with VITE_)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# First-party authentication for the event desk. These replace the temporary
# Vercel Authentication layer once proven, and they are the production API auth
# boundary: /api/sync-registration requires a valid operator session.
#
# Both values grant access, so neither may ever be VITE_ prefixed - everything
# so named is compiled into the browser bundle. Neither is ever sent to the
# browser, stored in IndexedDB, localStorage or sessionStorage, or logged.
#
# EVENT_OPERATOR_ACCESS_CODE
#   The shared desk code. Minimum 12 characters, compared EXACTLY - not
#   trimmed, not case-folded. Use a STRONG PASSPHRASE, not a 6-digit PIN: the
#   fixed 750ms delay on a wrong code is not a rate limiter.
#
# EVENT_SESSION_SECRET
#   HMAC-SHA256 signing key for operator sessions. Minimum 32 characters.
#   Rotating it INVALIDATES EVERY EXISTING SESSION immediately, which is how
#   sessions are revoked - there is no session store to clear.
#
# Sessions last 14 days so the desk is not asked to unlock again mid-event.

EVENT_OPERATOR_ACCESS_CODE=
EVENT_SESSION_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server only - central PostgreSQL (NEVER prefix this with VITE_)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Neon connection string for the central operational database: events,
# devices, device attributes and badge-range assignments.
#
# It is a CREDENTIAL. It must never be VITE_ prefixed - everything so named is
# compiled into the browser bundle - and it is never logged, never returned in
# an error and never sent to the client.
#
# Development and Production MUST NOT share a database branch. Point local
# development at a disposable Neon development branch, never at Production.
#
# Absent is a valid state today: no live workflow needs the database yet, so
# the application loads, authenticates, routes, registers devices, issues
# badges offline and syncs to Sheets without it. Only a deliberate database
# call fails, and it fails closed.
#
# Migrations are NEVER automatic. A human runs `pnpm db:migrate`; `pnpm
# db:check` is read-only. See docs/DATABASE.md.

DATABASE_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server only - ADMIN access (NEVER prefix these with VITE_)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A SEPARATE security realm from Operator Access above.
#
# Operator Access unlocks an event DESK: registration, holds, badge issuance.
# Admin Access unlocks the CONTROL PLANE at /admin: the central device
# registry, device attributes and badge-range assignment.
#
# Neither credential satisfies the other. An unlocked event device is not an
# Admin, and an Admin is not an operator. They use different cookies
# (__Host-navaratri_operator_session vs __Host-navaratri_admin_session) and
# different signing secrets.
#
# EVENT_ADMIN_ACCESS_CODE
#   Minimum 8 characters. That is a floor, not a recommendation: Admin can
#   create devices and assign badge ranges for the whole event, so a short
#   code leans on the edge rate limit (5 attempts/minute/IP) and the fixed
#   wrong-code delay. Prefer a passphrase well above the minimum. Compared
#   EXACTLY: not trimmed, not case-folded.
#
# EVENT_ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET
#   HMAC-SHA256 signing key for admin sessions. Minimum 32 characters.
#   Rotating it invalidates every admin session immediately.
#
#   Use a different value from EVENT_SESSION_SECRET. Realm separation does not
#   depend on it - admin signatures are domain-separated, so the two realms
#   reject each other's tokens even with identical secrets - but rotating one
#   realm should not disturb the other. Generate the two independently.
#
# Admin sessions last 12 hours, deliberately shorter than an operator session:
# Admin is a privileged online control plane, not a desk that must survive a
# multi-day event.
#
# Admin also requires DATABASE_URL. With any of the three absent, /admin fails
# closed with a clear message and the event application is unaffected.

EVENT_ADMIN_ACCESS_CODE=
EVENT_ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server only - DEVICE session (NEVER prefix this with VITE_)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A THIRD security realm, independent of Operator Access and Admin.
#
# A central device authenticates with eventSlug + loginName + password against
# POST /api/device-login and receives __Host-navaratri_device_session.
#
# None of the three realms satisfies another. A device session authorizes no
# Admin API and no operator API; an operator or Admin cookie authenticates no
# device. Each realm signs a different message, so even identical secrets keep
# them separate.
#
# EVENT_DEVICE_SESSION_SECRET
#   HMAC-SHA256 signing key for device sessions. Minimum 32 characters, taken
#   EXACTLY: not trimmed, not case-folded. Rotating it invalidates every
#   device session at once.
#
#   Generate it independently of EVENT_SESSION_SECRET and
#   EVENT_ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET. Realm separation does not depend on the values
#   differing - device signatures are domain-separated - but rotating one
#   realm should not disturb another.
#
# Device sessions last 14 days: an event desk may operate across several days.
# Per-device revocation is devices.session_version, not a session store -
# an Admin password reset increments it and every cookie issued under the old
# value stops verifying on its next online check.
#
# Device authentication also requires DATABASE_URL.
#
# PHASE 9C-B: this realm exists but the event application does not use it. The
# event routes still run on Operator Access, and holding a device cookie
# unlocks none of them. Leaving this unset changes nothing about the running
# application - device authentication simply reports itself unavailable.

EVENT_DEVICE_SESSION_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server only - offline device authorization PRIVATE key (NEVER VITE_)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The P-256 PRIVATE key that SIGNS offline authorization leases, as PKCS#8 DER
# in base64. Generated together with the public key above.
#
# PKCS#8 DER base64 rather than PEM so the value is a single line and survives
# a Vercel environment variable without newline handling.
#
# It is a SIGNING KEY. Anyone holding it can mint authorization for any device
# in the event, so it must never be VITE_ prefixed, never reach src/, never
# appear in an API response, in IndexedDB, in localStorage or in a log. Only
# the problem is ever logged, never any part of the value.
#
# A lease lasts at most 24 HOURS and never outlives events.ends_at:
#
#   exp = min(now + 24h, event.endsAt)
#
# An event that has already ended produces no lease at all. There is no
# indefinite offline authority and no local extension - only the server may
# issue one.
#
# WHAT A LEASE IS: signed LOCAL authorization, verified by the browser with
# the public key during an internet outage.
#
# WHAT IT IS NOT: a central API credential. No endpoint accepts it.
# /api/device-auth, /api/device-badge-claim, the Admin APIs and
# /api/sync-registration all keep requiring their own cookies.
#
# PHASE 9C-C3A: the lease is issued, verified and displayed, and unlocks
# NOTHING. /, /badge-registration and /device-registration still answer to
# Operator Access. Phase 9C-C3B is what wires it to event routes, and
# production configuration is required before that switch.

EVENT_DEVICE_OFFLINE_PRIVATE_KEY_PKCS8_B64=
